[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186162298 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y<1\\}.$\n\n![](images/01/01f3a8d99dd38ce2a728fe2cb4e4ab8de933ccd9ba27c87174779c428c303f48.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162300 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y<1\\}.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9cfac7f0f82088900ab5e603588a73d4932f08f592321c797a1fd94b8bafb4e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[c{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{14})]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4c}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4c}{21}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-86df2b6f-b298-58e2-a359-d3e3b918ef94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186247211 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},$ and the event $A=\\{X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/ea/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186247214 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/26/26cbd44827c3a5a3fde5d61b631ce4f214bcf9a36ce6bf76129b1fe9f9478eff.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-86df2b6f-b298-58e2-a359-d3e3b918ef94" :question/id #uuid "86df2b6f-b298-58e2-a359-d3e3b918ef94" :question/math-academy-id "q-111250" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>Y).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>Y\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/2d/2db85950b4d45901d79c173f264173d0c38046b2f087d0a28c8d5c69f024e31f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4504051-feaa-512b-9211-403c240f5138"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4504051-feaa-512b-9211-403c240f5138" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4504051-feaa-512b-9211-403c240f5138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1adf42e3-1c70-5503-860d-6962e69cc281" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18626b8c-2493-5d55-9463-bd9f28fb30c8" "answer-22d83ace-ef87-55ef-a2c4-9b139c05acd5" "answer-d7f6b48f-b583-53c7-8275-d83bc842c8f9" "answer-d5612858-da5d-5749-b4be-3afeccbaa11a" "answer-1adf42e3-1c70-5503-860d-6962e69cc281"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18626b8c-2493-5d55-9463-bd9f28fb30c8" :answer/id #uuid "18626b8c-2493-5d55-9463-bd9f28fb30c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22d83ace-ef87-55ef-a2c4-9b139c05acd5" :answer/id #uuid "22d83ace-ef87-55ef-a2c4-9b139c05acd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7f6b48f-b583-53c7-8275-d83bc842c8f9" :answer/id #uuid "d7f6b48f-b583-53c7-8275-d83bc842c8f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5612858-da5d-5749-b4be-3afeccbaa11a" :answer/id #uuid "d5612858-da5d-5749-b4be-3afeccbaa11a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1adf42e3-1c70-5503-860d-6962e69cc281" :answer/id #uuid "1adf42e3-1c70-5503-860d-6962e69cc281" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-14fa4da3-db56-52d2-9f88-608a139f9065" :activity/id #uuid "14fa4da3-db56-52d2-9f88-608a139f9065" :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049728 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-801fa807-3352-5bd1-814d-b25000e22228" "step-a5964aa6-3d4d-5b0d-8edc-acec2fd46d0d" "step-c59b87d3-3574-5bfd-8921-7d04759f0077" "step-e4e368b7-f8df-565c-b934-2de97cefe731" "step-778fed79-52b3-5022-a995-2a36a56152e4"] :activity/first-step "step-801fa807-3352-5bd1-814d-b25000e22228" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-801fa807-3352-5bd1-814d-b25000e22228" :step/id #uuid "801fa807-3352-5bd1-814d-b25000e22228" :step/content 17592186162298 :step/next "step-a5964aa6-3d4d-5b0d-8edc-acec2fd46d0d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a5964aa6-3d4d-5b0d-8edc-acec2fd46d0d" :step/id #uuid "a5964